<!--
  What Android is told about the game.

  ## Why GameActivity, and why there is now 21 KB of Java in here

  There is still no Java *source tree*. There is one compiled class,
  `com.google.androidgamesdk.GameActivity`, taken from Google's own
  `games-activity` release and turned into a `classes.dex` by `d8` —
  see `package-android.sh` and `README-games-activity.txt`.

  This used to be `android.app.NativeActivity`, which needs no Java at
  all, and the reason it is not any more is the on-screen keyboard.
  A NativeActivity's content view is a plain `View`: it does not
  implement `onCreateInputConnection`, so the input method is told the
  editor is `TYPE_NULL`, and what a `TYPE_NULL` editor is sent is key
  events. Key events carry keycodes, and **there is no keycode for
  `щ`** — so a game whose interface is in Russian, and whose font
  declares ASCII, the whole of Cyrillic and Polish diacritics, could
  not be typed into in three of its four alphabets. It was not a bug
  to be tuned out; NativeActivity cannot do it.

  `GameActivity` is a real activity whose view returns GameTextInput's
  `InputConnection`, so the input method commits whole text instead of
  pressing keys at us. The game reads that text through
  `AndroidApp::text_input_state` — see `platform::winit_backend`.

  The native half of GameActivity is compiled from C++ that the
  `android-activity` crate vendors and builds itself with `cc`; no
  Gradle is involved in any of this. The Java half has to match it
  exactly, which is why the version is pinned in two places at once.

  ## Why landscape only

  The world is drawn in a first-person view with a hotbar across the
  bottom and thumb controls in both corners. Held upright there is no
  room for the corners to be far enough apart to hold, and the view
  becomes a slot. Rotating is a thing the player would do once.
-->
<!--
  **The version code is the version name, and it only ever goes up.**
  major*10000 + minor*100 + patch: 1.5.1 is 10501. Play refuses an
  upload whose code is not greater than the last one, and a code that
  has been used is used forever -- there is no taking it back, which
  is why it is derived from the name rather than incremented by hand.
  The name is what a player reads; the code is what the store sorts
  by, and the two disagreeing is how a build nobody can update gets
  published.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    package="com.primitive.game"
    android:versionCode="10501"
    android:versionName="1.5.1">

    <!--
      24 is the floor everything this game needs shares: Vulkan arrived
      there, and so did the native-activity glue. See the note in
      `.cargo/config.toml`, which has to agree with this number — the
      library is *linked* against the API named there and *declared*
      here, and a build where those differ is one that installs and then
      cannot find a symbol.
    -->
    <uses-sdk android:minSdkVersion="24" android:targetSdkVersion="36" />

    <!-- Multiplayer. Singleplayer runs the server in-process and needs
         nothing, but the server browser is on the main menu either way. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />

    <!--
      Required, not merely preferred. A device that cannot do GLES 3.0
      cannot draw this world, and saying so here means the store never
      offers the game to one — which is better than the game installing
      and then showing a black screen.
    -->
    <uses-feature android:glEsVersion="0x00030000" android:required="true" />
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.touchscreen" android:required="true" />

    <!--
      `hasCode="true"`, and it is not optional now.

      It used to say `false`, which is the honest declaration for a
      package with no `classes.dex` in it — and with `false` the system
      never loads one, so `GameActivity` would be a class name that
      resolves to nothing and the app dies on launch with
      `ClassNotFoundException`. The dex is put in by `package-android.sh`
      after `aapt2 link`; this line is what makes Android look for it.
    -->
    <!--
      ## Why the package says it is a game, twice

      **Because the phone decides what this is, and acts on it.** On a
      device whose panel does 60, 90 and 120, the game ran at 60 under
      vsync however it asked otherwise: a window mode request
      (`preferredDisplayModeId`) was sent and refused, and a surface
      frame-rate request (`ANativeWindow_setFrameRate`) was *accepted*,
      logged as `status 0`, and changed nothing. The reason was one
      line of `adb shell settings get system peak_refresh_rate`: **60**.
      A system-wide ceiling no application can ask its way past.

      What can move that ceiling on this class of phone is the vendor's
      game mode, and the way in is the category. `appCategory` is the
      platform's own answer (API 26 and up) and is what a modern launcher
      and the battery accounting read. `isGame` is the older boolean; it
      is deprecated, and it is still what several vendor shells look at,
      so both are here rather than the tidier one alone.

      Neither is a promise from the platform, and this is not a
      performance fix it is the app answering a question it was
      previously silent about. The measurement that decides whether the
      refresh rate followed lives in the `[display]` lines the game
      already logs.
    -->
    <application
        android:label="Primitive"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:appCategory="game"
        android:isGame="true"
        android:hasCode="true"
        android:allowBackup="true">

        <!--
          ## What the on-screen keyboard is allowed to do to the game

          `windowSoftInputMode` below, and it is `adjustResize` now
          because GameActivity changed what the alternatives mean.

          Under NativeActivity this said `adjustPan|stateAlwaysHidden`.
          Panning needs a focused view to keep on screen and a
          NativeActivity has none, so nothing moved and the keyboard was
          simply drawn over the bottom of a full-size window — which
          suited a game that puts its own text fields wherever it likes.

          GameActivity *has* a focused view: that is the entire point of
          it. So `adjustPan` would now really pan, sliding the surface
          up by however much the input method decided, and the game
          would be drawing a world it no longer knows the position of —
          a hotbar off the bottom of the glass and touch coordinates
          that no longer match what is under the finger.

          `adjustResize` instead. The surface shrinks to the strip above
          the keyboard, which arrives as an ordinary `Resized` and goes
          through the same swapchain rebuild as a rotation — a path
          this game already has and already tests. The interface is laid
          out in its own space against the aspect it is given (see
          `widgets::Layout`), so the form re-lays itself into the strip
          and the field being typed into stays on screen, which is the
          whole requirement.

          `stateAlwaysHidden` is gone with it. It was there because the
          keyboard came up when a field was tapped and at no other time,
          and the system would otherwise put one back after a rotation.
          It is now actively wrong: with a served view and a real input
          connection, `stateAlwaysHidden` tells the system to suppress
          the window that `showSoftInput` is asking for, and the two
          requests fight — intermittently, which is worse than losing.
          The game raises and dismisses the keyboard itself, on the
          frame the focus changes; see the `AboutToWait` arm of the
          frame loop.

          ## Why a theme at all

          `NativeActivity` came with the framework's own bare theme.
          `GameActivity` extends `Activity` and gets the device default,
          which has a title bar and a status bar — so without this the
          game comes up with a strip of Android furniture across the top
          of a landscape world. `NoTitleBar.Fullscreen` is a framework
          style, which matters: a style of our own would need a
          `res/values` and a generated `R` class, and this package has
          neither.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name="com.primitive.game.PrimitiveActivity"
            android:label="Primitive"
            android:theme="@android:style/Theme.NoTitleBar.Fullscreen"
            android:screenOrientation="sensorLandscape"
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|screenSize|screenLayout|density|uiMode"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:exported="true">

            <!--
              Which library to load. `lib` is prepended and `.so`
              appended by the loader, so this names
              `lib/arm64-v8a/libprimitive.so` in the package.

              Read by `GameActivity.onCreate` rather than by the
              framework, but spelled exactly the same as NativeActivity
              spells it — the Java class was written to be a drop-in for
              it.
            -->
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.app.lib_name"
                android:value="primitive" />

            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
    </application>
</manifest>
